      <p class="location-intro">Decatur sits in a river valley that stays humid, and a lot of its prettiest homes were built when rainfall came slower and softer than it does now. Original gutters and downspouts that worked fine for eighty years choke on a modern cloudburst, and the constant damp does the rest. If your old-town home overflows every storm, or the gutters are pulling off wood that's been wet for a century, the problem is age and water — and both are fixable. Here's how gutters actually behave on these houses.</p>
