    <h2>Old Gutters, New Rainfall</h2>

    <p>The pre-1920 cottages and American Foursquares along Johnston Street, Sycamore, and the older blocks toward Austinville were built with half-round gutters and 2-inch round downspouts — a system sized for the rain of a hundred years ago. North Alabama now averages about 56 inches a year, and more of it arrives in hard, fast cells than it used to. A 2-inch round downspout simply can't pass that volume; it chokes on the first heavy cell of a storm and the gutter overtops. Homeowners assume the gutters are clogged. Often they're just outmatched by weather they were never designed for.</p>

    <p>The other factor is the valley itself. River-valley humidity keeps these gutters damp far longer than they'd stay in a drier climate, and organic silt plus shingle grit settles into a peat-like sludge that half-round gutters hold in the bottom radius. It stays wet, and aluminum sitting under constant moisture with oak-tannin runoff pits faster than most people expect. A gutter that's always damp ages differently than one that dries out between rains.</p>

    <h2>The Right Gutter Isn't Always the Cheapest One</h2>

    <p>Here's an honest admission the low bidder won't make: on a genuinely historic home, machine-run seamless aluminum in a modern K-style profile can look wrong and actually hurt resale in the older grid. The gutter that keeps up with modern rain and respects the architecture is usually 6-inch half-round in a heavier .032 aluminum or copper — not the .027 builder gauge, which oil-cans and sags on the long eaves of a two-story foursquare. It costs more. It's also the correct answer, and a contractor who quotes you the cheapest K-style for a 1910 home either doesn't know or doesn't care.</p>

    <p>If the house isn't in a sensitive block and you just want performance, 6-inch K-style with a 3x4 downspout upsizes the capacity and costs less than half-round. The point either way is capacity and correct material — not defaulting to whatever the crew has on the truck.</p>

    <h2>Why Gutters Fall Off Century-Old Houses</h2>

    <p>The single most common failure here is a gutter pulling clean off the fascia, and it's almost always the install, not the gutter. Crews reuse the original spike-and-ferrule holes and drive new spikes into fascia that's absorbed river-valley humidity for a hundred years. Punky old wood won't hold a spike — it works loose within a season and the run starts to sag and separate. The fix is switching to hidden hangers with a 2-1/2-inch screw driven into sound wood, and honestly replacing the fascia sections that crumble when you press on them. On these homes the fascia and trim repair is genuinely half the job, and a bid that doesn't mention it hasn't looked closely.</p>

    <p>Downspout discharge matters more on these houses too. Many old-town homes sit on brick or stone foundations with undersized or nonexistent footing drains, so water dumped at the base finds the basement or crawlspace fast in the humid months. Get the discharge at least 6 feet out from the wall. It's a small detail that saves a foundation.</p>

    <h2>Gutters Tie Into the Rest of the House</h2>

    <p>On an older Decatur home the gutter is rarely a standalone job. The fascia and roof edge age together, so a homeowner replacing gutters is often talking to a <a href="/locations/decatur/roofer/">Decatur roofer</a> about the drip edge and the last few courses of shingle at the same time — doing them together means the flashing actually integrates. The tannin streaks and organic film that river humidity leaves on an old gutter face are what a <a href="/locations/decatur/pressure-washing/">Decatur pressure washing</a> crew soft-washes without stripping the finish, and once the fascia is repaired, a <a href="/locations/decatur/painter/">painter</a> has to prime and seal that bare wood before it takes on moisture again. The <a href="/locations/decatur/">Decatur contractor overview</a> covers how these trades line up on an older home.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Gutter Work Costs in Decatur</h2>

    <p>Installed ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>6-inch K-style aluminum</strong> — about $9 to $12 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>6-inch half-round aluminum</strong> — about $14 to $20 per linear foot (heavier .032 gauge for the long eaves).</li>
      <li><strong>Copper half-round</strong> — about $28 to $40 per linear foot, for the homes that warrant it.</li>
      <li><strong>Fascia and trim repair</strong> — separate, and on a century-old home you should expect some.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A two-story foursquare with roughly 180 feet of half-round generally lands between $3,500 and $6,500 before fascia work. The material choice and the wood condition drive the whole number.</p>

    <h2>When to Do It</h2>

    <p>Between the heavy spring storm season and the humid depth of summer is the practical window — you want capacity in place before the hard rains and the fascia sealed before the wettest months work on any bare wood. If a gutter is already sagging or separating, don't ride it through another storm; the water it dumps behind the run is what accelerates the fascia rot you're trying to stop.</p>

    <h2>Questions Decatur Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Why do my old gutters overflow?</strong></p>
    <p>They're sized for century-old rainfall. A 2-inch round downspout can't move a modern cloudburst; upsize to 6-inch gutter with a 3x4 or a 3-inch round.</p>

    <p><strong>Is seamless aluminum right for a historic home?</strong></p>
    <p>Often not. Half-round in heavier aluminum or copper suits the architecture and holds up better on long eaves.</p>

    <p><strong>Why do gutters keep falling off?</strong></p>
    <p>Spikes reused in 100-year-old damp fascia. Switch to hidden hangers with 2-1/2-inch screws and replace the punky wood.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>$9 to $12 a foot for K-style, $14 to $20 for half-round, more for copper — plus fascia repair you should plan on.</p>

